This example supersedes the previous one of the same name. The difference is
the addition of recombination to the list of physics, for DEGAS 2 and 
for the UEDGE run that created our input plasma data. One 
consequence is that the region of low temperatures (~1 eV) is now quite
large and is spread across the target plate. For a variety of different
reasons, the differences between DEGAS 2 and EIRENE are greatest under
these conditions. In this example, we attempt to account for as many of
these differences as we can and achieve nearly statistical agreement between
the two codes. However, the process of doing this is quite involved and
is not recommended for users new to DEGAS 2.

Other files which are needed for this example are the same as in the
"Eirene_bench_difft_physics" run: eirene*.out, geometry_*.inp, 
ue_rect_slab.geom, uedata_rs_recom.u. 

At the time these examples were created, DEGAS 2 did not have extensive
surface physics data (apart from the relatively simple reflection coefficients
of DEGAS 2). Hence, the TRIM reflection data taken from EIRENE have been
used in this run as well as the "different physics" case. Nonetheless,
getting a good match between the two codes has required several adjustments
to the procedure (pmiwrite.web) used to read the TRIM data and write
out the reflection data into a netCDF file. The standard pmi.input
file in the "degas2/data" directory points to this file, D_refl_strim_Mo.nc.
Even then, one additional change to the main code must be made as well
(see below).

Next, the atomic physics between the two codes must be synchronized. The
collisional radiative data for electron impact ionization and recombination
with hydrogen were taken directly from EIRENE. The ratecalc code
was used with the input files hioneir.input and hreceir.input (which read
the Aladdin-format data files, eirhion.dat and eirhrec.dat, respectively) to
generate basic netCDF files. Manual changes, such as to the variable names,
had to be made manually to these files. Hence, they are included here
for convenience. If the data format (xsection.hweb) changes, these files
should be regenerated. For now, though, the reactions.input file will look
for them here and nothing more needs to be done.

The version of EIRENE being compared with also used older charge exchange
data with simple expressions for the momentum and energy transfers. 
The ratecalc code can be run with the input file hchexold.input to
generate the corresponding DEGAS 2 data. However, duplicating the
simple EIRENE expressions for the momentum and energy transfer rates
requires a hack either to ratecalc or to subroutine track_chargex in
the collide.web file. The former is preferred. For simplicity, the
hchex.nc file is included here and is pointed to by the reactions.input file.
A section at the end of this file describes the steps required to
regenerate it using ratecalc.

Both EIRENE and DEGAS 2 take their molecular reaction data from the 
"Janev book". EIRENE primarily uses the fits given in that book. As
described in the DATA_HISTORY file in the data directory, however, the
DEGAS 2 data have been taken from the corresponding DEGAS file (atmc.dat).
For this problem, one small difference between those two sets of data was
worth resolving. For the h2dis reaction, EIRENE happily evaluates the
reaction rate fit at the two lowest electron temperature values,
even though they are below the recommended minimum specified in the
Janev data. The DEGAS data had corrected values for these two points (and
others as well). To get a better comparison between EIRENE and DEGAS 2,
the DEGAS 2 data for h2dis must be hacked to match the EIRENE data.
Again for simplicity, we've included the h2dis.nc file here. Details on
recreating it can be found at the end of this file. In hchex.nc, the
value of sgvmax is left at its default value of 0.0 to effectively disable
rejection, as is done in the EIRENE run. 

Because this geometry differs from the normal single-null configuration
expected by readgeometry and readbackground, specific internal changes
were required to those codes. Before compiling readgeometry and 
readbackground:

 * Change the value of the flag GEOMETRY from GENERAL to UEDGE_RECT_SLAB 
   in both readgeometry.web and readbackground.web.

Be sure to reset those flags to GENERAL prior to running the code with other 
geometries.

Getting a good match between the code error estimates required changing
a few estimators from their default values. Here is the output of the
diff utility:

407,408c407,408
<  //      estimator[pr_rc_unknown]=tl_est_track
<          estimator[pr_rc_unknown]=tl_est_collision
---
>          estimator[pr_rc_unknown]=tl_est_track
>  //        estimator[pr_rc_unknown]=tl_est_collision
412,413c412,413
<          estimator[pr_rc_ionize_suppress]=tl_est_post_process
< //         estimator[pr_rc_ionize_suppress]=tl_est_track 
---
> //         estimator[pr_rc_ionize_suppress]=tl_est_post_process
>          estimator[pr_rc_ionize_suppress]=tl_est_track 
431,432c431,432
< //         estimator[pr_rc_dissociation]=tl_est_track
<          estimator[pr_rc_dissociation]=tl_est_collision
---
>          estimator[pr_rc_dissociation]=tl_est_track
> //         estimator[pr_rc_dissociation]=tl_est_collision

In each case, the estimator is being changed from "collision" or 
"post processing" to "track length". 

 * The first two set the estimators used for the 'ion source rate' (the
   same estimator is used for the other transfer terms as well). The
   tag "pr_rc_unknown" includes the charge exchange reaction;
   pr_rc_ionize_suppress corresponds to the hionize reaction (even
   though we are not using suppressed ionization here).

 * The last set of changes causes the track length estimator to be
   used for scoring momentum and energy transfers for molecular
   dissociation reactions (the track length estimator was already
   being used by default for the ion source rate).

This example will now also serve as a performance benchmark. However, some
aspects of this benchmark are not yet standard parts of the code (principally
because they result in increases in variance) and must be enabled by compiler 
switches:

 * geomint.hweb - PLANE_SURFACES 1 (normal is "0"; with "1", the code drops
   the quadratic terms in the surface representation).

 * collide.web - RR 1 (does Russian roulette when molecular dissociation
   products are both the same test species; with "0", both such products
   would be tracked).

Finally, getting good agreement required DEGAS 2 to duplicate EIRENE's
zeroing of the reflection yield for incident test particles of less than
1 eV energy. Presently, this is done via a hack:

 * flight.web - EIR_REC_BENCH 1 (the normal value of "0" skips the
   hacked code and the reflection routine uses the yield computed from the
   data; for the cases used here, all test species of less than 1 eV would
   have the yield computed for a 1 eV projectile, the lowest energy value
   in the data file).

Eventually, the data files and handling mechanisms should permit a variety
of options for extending data beyond their specified limits. 

The text and primary netCDF output here are for 80,000 (for each source
group) flights. 

 * To set the number of flights (after having run readbackground),
   edit the bk_uers.nc file in the run directory. Search for the variable
   "source_num_flights". This gives the number of flights for the recycling
   source group (first value) and for recombination (second value). Change
   the numbers to the desired values, save the file and exit the editor.

The results from a 1000 flight (for each source group) run are included here
as well in degas2_2000_out.nc and density_2000.out. The run
time on a Sun UltraSPARC 2 running SunOS 5.6 and V. 4.2 of f77 with
"-fast" optimization is 181 seconds (via the shell command "time flighttest").
The corresponding EIRENE run takes 153 seconds with standard deviations 
computed and printed (EIRENE runs considerably faster if the statistics
are not kept).

In addition to the EIRENE output files in the adjoining "different physics"
directory, we include here the terminal output from EIRENE for this
benchmark run, output_rec10b. The program matcheir was written to read
this file and compare results with the corresponding DEGAS 2 values. 
Since output_rec10b contains EIRENE's relative standard deviation values
and since its mean values DO NOT include the directly computed contributions 
from recombination (having them complicates interpretation of the 
relative standard deviations), we can quantitatively evaluate the
statistical agreement between the output of the two codes. To compare
the overall output, including all contributions from recombination,
use geomtesta and the eirene32.out and eirene44.out files.

GENERATING hchex.nc FILE

The changes required for ratecalc.web are outlined in this output from
the diff utility. Because the line numbers may have changed since this
file was written, a manual implementation of these hacks is recommended.
They appear in the "rank = 2" section of subroutine table_value.

1057c1057,1061
<             fit_coef[num_fit_coef_aladdin]=dble(zenergy)
---
>             if (zenergy > const(1.,-1)) then
>                fit_coef[num_fit_coef_aladdin]=dble(zenergy)
>             else
>                fit_coef[num_fit_coef_aladdin]=const(1.,-1)
>             end if
1061a1066,1072
>             if (j == 3) then
>                zsigma*=sqrt(two*zenergy*electron_charge/atomic_mass_unit)*const(1.,2)
>      $              *(one+half*ztemperature/zenergy)
>             else if (j == 4) then
>                zsigma*=two*const(1.,4)*electron_charge/atomic_mass_unit
>      $              *(zenergy+const(1.5)*ztemperature)
>             end if

Once ratecalc has been recompiled, the code can be run with hchexold.input:

    ratecalc hchexold.input

to regenerate hchex.nc.

These changes create values of the "I" integrals in the data file which
cause the usual momentum and energy transfer terms in subroutine 
track_chargex to give the same values as the simple expressions in
EIRENE. To see this, the following code can be hacked into track_chargex:

1351a1352,1353
>       real sd_mom1,sd_mom2,sd_mom3,sd_energy
> 
1392a1395,1408
>       sd_mom1=
>      $      -sp_m(pr_background(back))*rate*vb_flow[1]
>       sd_mom2=
>      $      -sp_m(pr_background(back))*rate*vb_flow[2]
>       sd_mom3=
>      $      -sp_m(pr_background(back))*rate*vb_flow[3]
>       sd_energy=
>      $      -(const(1.5)*independent_parameters[xs_var_temperature]
>      $       +half*sp_m(pr_background(back))*vc_abs2(vb_flow))*rate

For that matter, these expressions can be used to directly replace
the right-hand sides of the scoring_data values (only for the "reagent" 
section; the "product" section remains unchanged). Then, a simpler hchex.nc
with just the cross section and reaction rate can be used; the above
described hacks to ratecalc are not required.

CREATING THE h2dis.nc FILE

The differences between this file and the one in the data directory are
small. Hence, one should just copy that file to a working directory and
make the following changes. The line numbers here should not be trusted.
However, the numbers themselves should be exactly the same. Note that
the first two lines deal with the reaction rate; the last four with the
electron energy loss rate (the reaction rate times 10.5 eV).

402c402
<  xs_data_tab = 7.8572e-13, 6.92383e-12, 3.86312e-11, 1.49274e-10, 
---
>  xs_data_tab = 1.07603e-12, 7.55471e-12, 3.86312e-11, 1.49274e-10, 
411,412c411,412
<     1.04559e-11, 1.63074e-11, 3.07299e-11, 7.21099e-11, 3, 8.25006e-12, 
<     7.270021e-11, 4.056276e-10, 1.567377e-09, 4.7336205e-09, 1.1522805e-08, 
---
>     1.04559e-11, 1.63074e-11, 3.07299e-11, 7.21099e-11, 3, 1.1298315e-11, 
>     7.9324455e-11, 4.056276e-10, 1.567377e-09, 4.7336205e-09, 1.1522805e-08, 
